<p class="whitespace-normal break-words">Margulis first presented her ideas in a 1967 paper titled "On the origin of mitosing cells" published in the Journal of Theoretical Biology. In this work, she proposed that organelles like mitochondria and chloroplasts were once free-living bacteria that became permanent residents inside host cells through endosymbiosis.</p>
<p class="whitespace-normal break-words">While the concept of organisms living inside other organisms had been observed before, Margulis was the one who formalized the terminology and developed the comprehensive theory explaining how this process led to the evolution of complex eukaryotic cells. Her ideas were initially met with skepticism but are now widely accepted as a fundamental principle in evolutionary biology.</p></div></div><div class="flex flex-col gap-2 py-2"></div></div><div class="absolute bottom-0 right-2 pointer-events-none" style="transform: none;"><div class="rounded-lg transition min-w-max pointer-events-auto translate-x-2 opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100"><div class="text-text-300 flex items-stretch justify-between"><button class="inline-flex
